WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.220
Data collection architecture is only one
vulnerability point.

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.780
The structural integrity of the peer
review process presents a different set

3
00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:11.320
of risks. Take Professor Daniels, a
sociologist leading an interdisciplinary

4
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.080
research team that relies on social media
platforms for data collection.

5
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.720
Her team is actively drafting a manuscript
based on innovative empirical

6
00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.700
methods they have recently developed.

7
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.220
While drafting, she receives an invitation
from an academic journal to peer

8
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.340
review a newly submitted external
manuscript.

9
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.480
Upon reading the abstract, she discovers a
critical complication.

10
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.400
The external manuscript contains
significant direct overlap with her team's

11
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.520
own unsubmitted research. The ethical
standard requires immediate recusal.

12
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:46.600
Instead, Professor Daniels accepts the
invitation to review.

13
00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:51.100
Ignoring a conflict of interest at this
stage fractures the firewall of the

14
00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:54.520
peer review system before a single word is
even read.

15
00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.940
Citing lack of time, Professor Daniels
delegates reading the manuscript to her

16
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.500
doctoral student. By sharing the text, she
breaks the strict pre-publication

17
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.640
confidentiality guaranteed to the
submitting authors.

18
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.380
When the student finishes, Professor
Daniels submits the highly critical

19
00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:13.700
review back to the journal without ever
reading it herself.

20
00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:17.240
The submitted text contains a final
ethical breach.

21
00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:21.680
The review demands major revisions,
including a coercive mandate that the

22
00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.320
authors cite Professor Daniels' own
references.

23
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.520
This flow-tert breaks down the structural
compounding of these failures.

24
00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.280
The first violation is the conflict of
interest inherent in reviewing

25
00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.520
competing work, which biases the
evaluation before it even begins.

26
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.060
The second is unauthorized delegation,
crossing a struct ethical boundary by

27
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.640
handing a confidential manuscript to a
student.

28
00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:48.360
The third is coercive citation, an abuse
of academic power used to demand

29
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.520
self-references and artificially inflate
impact metrics.

30
00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:56.780
A routine workflow pressure, a lack of
time, was used to justify weaponizing a

31
00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.960
student and compromising a competing
team's research.
